# Planning Poker Project - Custom Instructions

## Special Commands
- Ask Cline to "initialize memory bank" to activate project-specific knowledge and capabilities

## Project Overview
This is a Planning Poker application built with Next.js, React, TypeScript, and Firebase. Planning Poker is an agile estimation technique used by teams to estimate effort or relative size of development tasks.

## Tech Stack
- Next.js (React framework)
- TypeScript
- Tailwind CSS for styling
- Firebase for real-time database and authentication

## Code Organization
- `/src/app`: Next.js app router pages and layouts
- `/src/component`: React components
  - `/lib`: Reusable UI components
  - `/PokerRoom`: Components specific to the poker room functionality
- `/src/provider`: Context providers and Firebase integration
  - `/event`: Custom hooks for event handling
  - `/util`: Firebase database utilities
- `/src/util`: Utility functions

## Development Guidelines

### Component Structure
- Follow the pattern of having index.tsx files that re-export components
- Keep components focused on a single responsibility
- Use TypeScript interfaces for props

### State Management
- Use Firebase for shared state across users
- Use React context for application-wide state
- Use local state for component-specific state

### Styling
- Use Tailwind CSS for styling
- Follow the existing design patterns

### Firebase Integration
- Follow the schema defined in schema/firebase-schema.json
- Use the FireBaseProvider for database operations
- Implement proper error handling for all Firebase operations

## Common Tasks
- To add a new feature, create components in the appropriate directory
- For UI components, add them to src/component/lib
- For business logic, use the provider pattern in src/provider
- Test changes in the poker room at /room/poker/[id]

## Best Practices
- Write clean, self-documenting code
- Add appropriate TypeScript types
- Follow the existing patterns in the codebase
- Keep components small and focused
- Use meaningful variable and function names
- Implement proper error handling
